r  one  horror book on amazon and she  subsequently  got a contract with simon schuster i  believe which has published some of her  like kind of bigger books  and um you know so she's kind of very  well known in the horror community  now that said um i hadn't read any of  her stuff so i wanted to  kind of get started with you know  reading all of her  work so i wasn't really sure where to  start now i do  i'm gonna say like right out of the gate  i do prefer to have  physical copies of the books that i'm  going to review and i do actually have  physical copies of a lot of the ones  that i'm going to review and i've  ordered a lot of them as well so i can  you know kind of show them on the show  because i just  i prefer to read actual paper books i  know that's weird but um  and i like to be able to like show them  on the show but this one  um was actually on because i have a  kindle unlimited subscription  so this one of her books was available  uh for free as kindle unlimited  so i decided to start with this one so i  read the ebook version  which means that um you know i don't  have a book to show you so  you know i'll just put it i'll put it  like over there over there  wherever i don't know where i'm gonna  put it this book is called  if you see her now this came out in 2019  and even though i guess that she has  like i said you know a contract with  simon and schuster who have published  like some of her better known books  this one i believe she published on her  own  now she says in the back of the book  that this one like she kind of got  started on it and then i guess she had a  baby and like so obviously she didn't  have a lot of time to finish a book  um so finally she got it done and i  guess she felt like she wanted to put it  out on her own  so that's kind of what ended up  happening so this is actually one of her  more  recent books i don't think it's her  newest one because i think she had one  out this year maybe  but um it is one of her more recent ones  so i decided that this would be a good  one to start with and you know the  synopsis sounded kind of cool so the  synopsis of this one  this basically follows a guy named jesse  wells  and he lives in this little like  [ __ ] nowhere town  uh called warsaw michigan and um you  know he's one of those guys where  you know he wants to be a writer and  he's like you know  i guess i don't want to say like smarter  than anybody else in town but he kind of  like has larger ambitions  from this little town and wants to get  the hell out of there now  the book starts in i believe 2000 or  2001  when uh jesse and his two friends whose  names are casey and reed  they're in high school or they're  getting ready to graduate from high  school  and what ends up happening is that i  guess there's this creepy old house  um like sort of out beyond the edges of  the town  and it kind of has all these reputation  like you know about um you know suicides  murders like all kind of  you know ghost stories and [ __ ] like  that about it you're just your typical  like haunted house type of thing out at  the edge of town  so they um decide that  they're gonna like have a few drinks and  they're gonna drive  out there uh you know just to kind of  hang out or whatever now  it comes to pass that jesse  has had a bad experience there when he  was a kid i believe he was 11 years old  and i don't think he told the other guys  about it um  but i guess reed who's the guy that's  driving out on this excursion  he's kind of become obsessed with this  house since like all he does is like  think about it and talk about it and  sometimes he goes and drives and like  sits in front of it in his car and  everything  so so they're driving out there and  jesse is like really not happy about it  because he didn't know that that's where  they were going to be going  so they end up going to the house and  you know it's this is going to have  light spoilers i'm not  really gonna spoil anything that wasn't  uh  you know in the description of the book  that was like on goodreads and stuff  like that but  if i do actually happen to spoil things  i've you know i'm sorry about that but  i'm just saying it's probably mild  spoilers i'm not gonna tell you the end  or anything  um which will probably be par for the  course with all the book reviews because  it's hard to talk about them if you  can't talk about what happened in the  book  to a certain extent so if you want to  come at things  like just completely blind then you know  you might want to turn this off and  come back to it later like after you've  read the book so  they end up going out to the house um  casey and reed  go up because casey is like a  photographer and he's like fascinated  with abandoned  buildings and whatnot so they go inside  the house but jesse kind of chickens out  and he stays outside  now when they go in there it turns out  that reed  goes up to like the fourth floor of the  mansion and  apparently jumps off or commits suicide  at least that's the story  so that's kind of like a traumatic thing  that happened  like in the past so then we come forward  to 2019  where you know jesse is the main  character and casey are all grown up  jesse has uh not been able to  um realize his dream of becoming a  writer and he's working as an english  teacher  in the same high school that he  graduated from in the same small town  and he's kind of not happy about it uh  he did marry his  uh you know high school sweetheart or  whatever which he's happy about that and  they've just had a son  but he's kind of like bummed out that  he's still stuck in this town and he's  you know he was going to be the next  stephen king he said and it's like he  never really got anywhere he never  finished a novel he never did anything  like that  so when you get when we go forward to  the present  then casey the other guy that was like  his friend that was there when reed  supposedly committed suicide  he comes back to the house and he's been  like acting  he's acting really weird and he  basically says to jesse okay well we  have to go back to that house  um to kind of get closure you know and  he's just acting really bizarre and  jesse doesn't want to go  obviously but he does end up going  much to his detriment let's uh say that  and that's probably as much of the plot  as i'm going to give away  now what i will say about this book  is that it's a really i read it in like  two days because it was one of those  things where like when i was reading it  i was like yeah this is like pretty good  and stuff like that but then when i  wasn't reading it i kept thinking about  it like man i should  get back to that because i wanted to see  what would happen so  she does have a really good way of  getting the story moving getting you  invested in it like  really quickly and getting you to really  just  want to keep reading it you know i mean  it's like you did i didn't really want  to put it down  and i finished it really really quickly  um  i will say too that this is it gave me i  don't know if all of her books are like  this because this is the first one of  hers that i've  read i'm going to review some other ones  too because i actually do have a print  version of one of her books  that i'm going to review eventually so i  don't know if all of them are like that  it's very it's  stephen king i guess not as like  gory or like oh because i don't want to  say like over the top but it's not  it's a little more like low-key than  stephen king i guess so it's like kind  of like  low-key stephen king and particularly  the main character  in this book if you see her jesse wells  he reminded me very much of jack torren  it's not from the movie but from the  novel  in the sense that he was you know  in a teacher who was aspiring to be a  writer but was kind of like perceived  himself as a failure  um he was also an alcoholic um at some  point like when he was younger but had  been sober for a very long time but it  was still something that was  haunting him and he still had like a lot  of demons and stuff  and in this story as it goes on and he  starts to become  obsessed with what's going on in this  house and having visions about what's  going on in this house and  kind of developing this obsession where  he has to like write about it  and what he thinks happened there  because he starts having visions about  this little girl  and um that's really scary and he just  like wants to get to the bottom of the  mystery of like what happened there even  though he thinks he's making it up and  there's like all this other stuff going  on or you know and you don't know if  it's like  just all him making it up or if it's  like something that actually happened  there that he's like tapping into how  somehow  but as it goes on and as he becomes more  you know tied up with this house and  like obsessed with his novel  he goes he starts going very much like  jack torrance's character where he  starts like descending into madness he  starts like  getting really self-destructive he  starts like  lashing out at people around him like  his wife and uh you know  his friends and things and so it it gave  me that kind of vibe like big time and  also  the kind of um it's you know it's not  quite as sprawling as like a stephen  king story because stephen king tends to  like have like a whole bunch of  characters  uh whereas this one was very much just  focused on uh jesse wells and you're  in his head most of the time um you know  there's other characters that are  important but he's kind of the main one  and you're  you're seeing everything from his point  of view um  i will say and i and i saw this in a lot  of other reviews of this book that the  main character  of jesse wells was kind of unlikable or  was hard to root for  i didn't find him particularly unlikable  you know and i didn't have a problem  like relating to him  although i will say that he was a  frustrating character but i think he was  meant to be a frustrating character i  mean he had his own  demons with alcoholism he had his own  demons of  you know just feeling like a failure in  life and trying to find like writing  inspiration  and not really being able to do it he  you know yes he was  depressive yes he was you know sometimes  he should have asked for help or he  should have told  his wife or his friends or something  like that what was going on rather than  keeping everything to himself  um but it didn't bother me that much  because  you know even though it's frustrating in  terms of the story  it very much was in accordance with his  character  because that's very much what he was  like he didn't want to ask for help he  wanted to be able to  sort things out on his own and not  burden other people maybe so so i didn't  really find that  a problem and like i said i didn't find  him particularly unlikable although i  can see how  some readers might um this also gave me  a little bit  of uh an exercise excercisty kind of  vibe too  and like because there's some kind of  demon possession kind of stuff going on  which i liked but you know it's not  super original  like i said this is this is basically a  haunted house  demon possession ghost kind of story and  like descendant of madness kind of story  so it's like stephen king  sort of shining ye with some exorcisty  kind of vibe to it  but not as like overtly  crazy it's more like creepy more dread  inducing more  it's not like overtly horror there's  like some action in it there's you know  stuff like that but  it's not kind of like these big action  set pieces and it's very much focused on  just this  main character and a couple of other uh  you know characters around him  which actually i kind of like um i liked  it better that was kind of a more  intimate  story now just from a very nitpicky  because i'm really anal retentive about  grammar  uh if you know me you will know this i  you know i don't know like if she had an  outside editor on this one or whatever  but there was a couple of typos in this  which that's not a big deal like a typo  but one thing that bothered me that  probably wasn't a typo because it  happened at least four or five times  was that she would write that somebody  had lied when they met when she met like  had lay  you know what i mean like the where the  body had lay  uh she would say where the body had lied  and she did that like  a cup a few times and it always kind of  like maybe go  wait what so it always kind of like drew  me out of the story which is really  weird because  you know everywhere else like the  english is like perfectly  cromulent it was you know it was like  perfectly fine but then  there was just that and it happened like  more than once so it really really  bugged me because i was like had lied  did you like read that out loud it's  like that's not how dear swiss is it was  you know  so that kind of bothered me a little bit  too but like i said that's very very  very nitpicky  um i will say that the ending of this  was very  um bleak which i dig that i like that  you know i if it can have a happy ending  too i don't really care it's as long as  it kind of makes sense in the course of  the story  but i'm not gonna like be all like one  of those people that is like oh i don't  like bleak endings or  anything like that because um i don't  mind them at all  and i think in the context of this story  um i think it might have been like the  only  possible ending um you know with the  mythology that she had constructed  because what you know before it ended i  was sitting there thinking well i don't  know how you're gonna  fix this you know what i mean like i  didn't know how he's gonna fix it so i  kind of like that now  from what i've heard like i said this is  the first book of hers that i've read  um but i've had her have heard other  people talking about her books that she  um often does do rather bleak endings so  just you know  fyi 